thers who write for  the Yale Daily News make the most of the  extraordinary variety of people and  opportunities that are available to you  here so far my advice to use is focused  entirely on how you might get the most  out of your Yale education you might be  wondering am I here just to exploit all  of Yale's treasures for myself alone  well the answer is no we have confidence  based on the evidence of history and  knowledge of the culture of this place  that your journey towards self-discovery  your progress toward finding your  passion will yield more than self  gratification and personal advancement  we believe that because you are  intelligent  reflective members of a community of  scholars that you will come to recognize  that with the abundant opportunities for  self enrichment that Yale provides there  also come responsibilities and what are  these responsibilities they begin with  responsibility for the well-being of the  institution that you are joining today  let me remind you that even for those of  you whose parents are paying the full  tuition room and board charges more than  half the total cost of your Yale  education is supported by gifts of those  who came before you more than half of  you hold scholarships and most of our  buildings athletic facilities museum and  library collections trace to gifts from  graduates of Yale College your  responsibilities also include good  citizenship in its many varieties at  Yale's founding this took the form of  supporting the young New Haven colony  and the Congregational Church today  while volunteer service to local  community organizations both secular and  religious remains a distinguishing  characteristic of Yale graduates our  horizons have broadened some of you will  undoubtedly carry on Yale's great  tradition of producing national leaders  and for all of you who spend most of  your adult lives in the United States  there is an emerging burden of  citizenship that will be yours to bear  and that is the powerfully important  burden of helping to raise the level of  public discourse and making our  democracy work one has only to compare  the rhetoric of today's leaders with the  speeches of Abraham Lincoln given 150  years ago or with the transcripts of the  kennedy-nixon debates of 50 years ago to  see how oversimplified ideology and  appeal to narrow interest groups have  triumphed over intelligence and  moderation  in public discussion by insisting as  citizens on serious discussion instead  of slogans that mask narrow partisan  interests you can help make our  democracy more effective today because  the world is so highly interconnected  and interdependent you will have the  added responsibility of acting as global  citizens your generation more than any  that has gone before will need deep  knowledge of an intimate engagement with  cultures and societies very different  from your own those of you who come from  abroad will of course experience  immersion in another culture right here  in New Haven the rest of you may do so  by taking advantage of one or more of  our many programs of work or study  abroad such an experience will stretch  you in just the way I'm recommending  more generally it will force you to see  yourself from a different perspective  and to see others free from  preconceptions since so many of the  issues confronting us from poverty and  disease to the proliferation of nuclear  weapons require cooperative  international solutions a cross-cultural  perspective is invaluable even before  you travel overseas you might start by  preparing yourself yourselves for global  citizenship by sampling some of the  courses in international studies offered  by our recently established Jackson  Institute such as the new  multidisciplinary gateway course on  global affairs now in addition to these  burdens of local national and  international citizenship that I've  mentioned your generation will need to  rise as well to the challenge of  planetary stewardship without a radical  reduction in emissions of greenhouse  gases too  during your lifetimes much of humanity  will suffer dislocation and famine on an  unprecedented scale we have both the  current means to slow down the  accumulation of atmospheric carbon and  the imagination to develop the  technologies needed to prevent  catastrophe we seem to lack only the  conviction and collective will you of  course will need to scrutinize the  evidence for yourselves with all the  critical intelligence that you can  muster but if you do I'm confident that  you will assume this last responsibility  as well and you'll have the opportunity  to practice planetary stewardship right  here at Yale as we try to model what it  means to become a sustainable campus  women and men of the class of 2014 we  take the greatest pleasure in welcoming  you here to Yale College and we delight  in the anticipation of the opportunities  that you will seize and that there and  the responsibilities that you will come  to shoulder as citizens of your  communities the nation the world and the  planet welcome to Yale
 

